WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Venus will dominate evening skies this August, while Mercury and Jupiter form a tight dawn pairing. NASA has assigned astronaut Dennis Burnham to her first space station mission. She will fly to the International Space Station next spring aboard the Ross Cosmos Soyuz MS-30 spacecraft. Burnham, a former NASA intern, will make her first trip to orbit on the mission. The assignment adds another crew member to the steady rotation of international partners visiting the station.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:44.840
NASA released the news alongside details on her background and training path. Watch for further crew announcements as the launch window approaches. From one astronaut's path to orbit, we turn to the maintenance work that keeps the station running.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:55.760
Flight Director's outline three spacewalks scheduled outside the International Space Station during August. The outings will focus on maintenance and science hardware tasks.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:08.520
Live coverage of the preview briefing is available through NASA channels. Crews are preparing equipment and timelines ahead of the first excursion. The schedule keeps station operations on track through the summer.

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:24.420
While crews handle station upkeep, the night sky offers its own seasonal maintenance in the form of shifting planets. Venus will stand out as the dominant evening planet through the month. Mercury and Jupiter will appear close together in the pre-dawn sky.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:36.320
Observers can track these positions with basic equipment or the naked eye under clear conditions. The changing geometry offers repeated viewing opportunities across different latitudes.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:52.860
Charts map the planet's paths night by night. Mercury's current brightening phase gives us a closer look at the innermost planet itself. Mercury has reached magnitude 0.5 and continues to brighten ahead of its greatest western elongation.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:02:02.580
The planet sits low in the early morning sky and should remain visible for several more days. Observers are encouraged to look shortly before sunrise.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:17.860
The current elongation provides one of the better chances to spot the innermost planet this year. Daily updates track its changing position and brightness. Mercury's weak magnetic field adds another layer to why the planet behaves the way it does.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:32.400
Mercury's weak magnetic field periodically captures and funnels solar wind particles toward the surface. The field was first detected in 1974 and continues to surprise researchers given the planet's small size.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:41.860
Data show the field creates temporary trapping regions rather than a steady shield. These interactions differ from Earth's more robust magnetosphere.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:54.860
Future orbiter measurements will map the variability in greater detail. From our own solar systems extremes, we move to a nearby galaxy that serves as a natural laboratory for star formation.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:09.060
The James Webb Space Telescope is examining binary stars inside the small Magellanic Cloud. The galaxy's lower metalicity offers a natural laboratory for testing how metal content affects stellar pairs.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:20.700
Early data already showed differences in mass transfer and evolution compared with Milky Way systems. The observations target young binaries still embedded in their birth clouds.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:36.820
Results will help refine models of star formation across varying galactic environments. If the sun formed inside a galaxy like the small Magellanic Cloud, it would have started with only one fifth the iron and other heavy elements we measure today.

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:52.380
That scarcity changes everything about how a star sheds its outer layers and how two stars in a pair exchange mass. In metal-poor gas, stellar winds are weaker, so stars retain more mass until they expand and fill their roast lobes.

00:03:52.380 --> 00:04:06.060
A typical closed binary might then transfer material at rates 10 times higher than equivalent Milky Way pairs, driving rapid spin-up or even envelope ejection on time scales of just a few hundred thousand years.

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:18.280
We can watch the outcome in ultraviolet and infrared light, yet we still cannot predict whether the same low metal conditions produce more neutron star mergers or more stripped envelope supernovae.

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:30.360
The unanswered piece is whether these altered pathways also change the final compact object mass distribution in ways that affect gravitational wave signals we detect from across the universe.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:43.280
Stars and stellar remnants already account for the vast majority of a galaxy's barionic mass, so shifts in binary outcomes could reshape how entire galaxies evolve over cosmic time.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:58.280
While JWST peers at distant binaries, closer to home, experts are warning about traffic in cis lunar space. Proposed lunar missions from multiple agencies are increasing traffic between Earth and the Moon.

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:11.000
Experts warn that debris in cis lunar space could threaten both robotic and crude spacecraft. Current tracking focuses mainly on low Earth orbit, leaving higher regions less monitored.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:19.940
The article outlines gaps in coordination and data sharing. International discussions on traffic rules are described as increasingly urgent.

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:26.020
Orbital congestion concerns extend beyond the Moon to propose data center constellations.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:33.980
Companies planning large constellations of data center satellites face new questions about collision avoidance and spectrum use.

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:44.840
Experts recommend developing safety protocols before the first systems reach orbit. The article notes gaps in current licensing frameworks for non-traditional payloads.

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:53.840
Early coordination could reduce later conflicts with existing operators. Industry and regulatory groups are beginning preliminary discussions.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:06:07.580
From future infrastructure in orbit, we return to striking images of our own planet captured this week. A radar image from the Copernicus Sentinel-1 mission shows New Orleans and the Mississippi River Delta in detail.

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:17.640
The instrument penetrates cloud cover to map the river's sediment patterns and coastal features. The Delta ranks among North America's largest river systems.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:28.100
Repeated passes allow scientists to monitor changes in wetlands and navigation channels. The image adds to the mission's archive of dynamic coastal regions.

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:43.260
Earth imagery leads naturally to beautiful deep sky views released this week. The daily astronomy picture features the globular cluster NGC-4372 alongside the dark doodad nebula.

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:52.920
The cluster lies in the southern constellation Muska. The dark nebula appears as a striking lane of obscuring dust against the background stars.

00:06:53.740 --> 00:07:05.380
The image combines data that reveals both the dense star field and the foreground dust structure. Such pairings illustrate how dust and star clusters interact along the Milky Way plane.

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:21.440
From nearby star clusters, we look at processes that shut down star formation in the universe's youngest galaxies. JWST observations reveal processes that appear to shut down star formation in some of the universe's youngest galaxies.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:31.560
Compact objects lose gas reservoirs faster than models predicted. The findings come from infrared imaging that penetrates dust in distant systems.

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:43.800
Astronomers compare the results against simulations to identify which feedback mechanisms dominate. Follow-up spectra will test whether outflows or other processes are responsible.

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:56.560
From galaxy scale quenching, we turn to a single black hole that refused to settle down quickly. New observations captured a black hole outburst followed by extended changes in surrounding material.

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:08.900
The event was led by Warwick postdoctoral fellow Dr. Noel Castro Segura. Data indicate the system retained heated gas and altered accretion patterns long after the initial flair.

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:18.020
The findings challenge simple models of rapid return to quiescence. Additional monitoring will track how the disk settles over coming months.

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:29.380
From cosmic extremes to hands-on student research closer to home, participants in NASA's student airborne research program released ozone sands from the Texas Gulf Coast.

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:38.620
The balloons carried instruments to measure ozone profiles through the lower atmosphere. Windy conditions tested the team's handling skills during launch.

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:52.620
The flights formed part of the program's final summer session that ended July 27. Data will contribute to regional air quality studies. Student field work on earth contrasts with satellite views of recent wildfires.

00:08:53.700 --> 00:09:07.380
Satellite views document extensive fires across Spain and France during recent heat waves. Prolonged drought left vegetation highly flammable, imagery shows burn scars and smoke plumes moving with prevailing winds.

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:21.640
The observations help map fire progression and assess damage to forested areas. Similar data support ongoing efforts to understand drought fire linkages. From earth monitoring, we close with one more skywatching note.

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:34.260
Vega shines as the brightest star in the summer triangle visible overhead in July evenings across the northern hemisphere. Astronomers have studied the star extensively because of its brightness and proximity.

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:48.440
It ranks among the most observed objects in the summer sky. Historical records show people have tracked Vega for thousands of years. The star continues to serve as a reference point for calibration and observation programs.

00:09:48.440 --> 00:10:04.980
Keep an eye on further details about the three scheduled August space walks as crews finalize timelines. That's fascinating frontiers for today. If you enjoyed this, a rating or review on Apple podcasts or Spotify really helps new listeners find the show.

